-- DICT-SEMANTICS §3 W3, the DEFAULT-BODY half. W3 says the rule applies to the
-- impl's body "or by the class's DEFAULT, which is checked by this same rule (it
-- is the class-provided impl, at the head `C ā_C` itself with `ā_C` also held
-- rigid)".
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived): `mk : a -> b` quantifies `b` at the method level.
-- A default body of `42` needs `Num b`, which the declared scheme does not
-- license, and `b` belongs to every caller. W3 rejects -- and it must reject the
-- DEFAULT specifically, with `ā_C` rigid too, since a default is checked once at
-- the interface rather than per impl.
--
-- OBSERVED: check/run/build all REJECT with `T-IMPL-TOO-SPECIFIC`, and the
-- message is default-specific: "DEFAULT BODY of 'mk' in interface 'Mk' requires
-- 'Num' on the method's quantified type variable 'b' …". CONFORMANT.
--
-- Why this row exists separately from the two impl-body W3 rows: they check
-- `checkImplMethodRigidity`, this checks `checkDefaultMethodRigidity` -- distinct
-- sites, and the corpus previously had NO fixture declaring a default body inside
-- an `interface` at all, so the impl rows alone would have let a default-only
-- regression through while reading as "W3 is covered". Added after an adversarial
-- review (F2) found the gap was neither covered nor declared.
--
-- NOTE there is deliberately no `impl` here: the default body is rejected on the
-- INTERFACE declaration alone, which is the point -- W3 on a default is not
-- contingent on any instance existing.
interface Mk a where
  mk : a -> b
  mk _ = 42

main = println 0
